  <steps mode="directional">
    <step order="0">Context + rigor declaration. Re-verify the two line references at HEAD (`ExpiryPredicate`, `ConfersAccessOn`) and re-run the `grep "sprk_expiresdate eq null"` census — if it returns more than the one line, STOP and report, because a second hand-rolled copy would mean the hoist has already been broken by another project.</step>
    <step order="1">Invert BOTH definitions in one edit: drop the `eq null` branch from `ExpiryPredicate`, and change `ConfersAccessOn` so a null expiry confers nothing. Update both doc comments — they currently EXPLAIN the old rule, and a comment that outlives its mechanism is this project's AP-12.</step>
    <step order="2">Add a test that pins the two definitions to the SAME semantics for the null case, so they cannot drift apart again. Then invert the characterization tests that assert the retired rule, renaming any whose name states it, each with a one-line reason citing D-1.</step>
    <step order="3">Perturbation (mandatory): restore the `eq null` branch and confirm the new tests go RED; restore the mirror's `is null ||` and confirm the agreement test goes RED independently. Revert both. Report the exact counts before and after.</step>
    <step order="4">Docs: fix entity-schema.md's stale "NOT IMPLEMENTED" note; add the pre-deploy COUNT command to EXTERNAL-ACCESS-ADMIN-SETUP.md, stating plainly that every undated active grant loses access on deploy and that the count must be taken and reviewed BEFORE the deploy, not after.</step>
    <step order="5">Build + full external-access suite green; publish size reported vs a fresh master build. Write notes/task-107-grant-expiry-read-default.md. Leave TASK-INDEX.md and this POML's status to the orchestrator.</step>
  </steps>

  <escalation>
    <trigger>If the `sprk_expiresdate eq null` census returns MORE than the single `ExpiryPredicate` line, STOP — a second copy means the single-definition invariant is already broken, and fixing that is a prerequisite, not a side effect of this task.</trigger>
    <trigger>If closing ISS-009 appears to need a Dataverse-side mechanism after all, STOP and escalate to the owner. D-1 ruled plugins out repo-wide; re-opening that is the owner's call, never an implementation decision.</trigger>
    <trigger>🔔 If the live COUNT of undated active grant rows cannot be taken (no Dataverse access this session), deliver the code + tests + docs and state EXPLICITLY that the pre-deploy gate is unmet. Do NOT claim "blast radius zero" on the strength of the D-1 note — that claim was about live data and has not been re-measured.</trigger>
  </escalation>

  <justification>
    <existing>`ExpiryPredicate` + `ConfersAccessOn` already exist as the single hoisted definition pair; this task changes their semantics, it does not add a component.</existing>
    <extension>Yes — an in-place semantic change to two adjacent existing members. No new service, no new mechanism, no new surface. This is why option A was chosen over B/C for the read half.</extension>
    <cost-of-doing-nothing>A grant row created outside the BFF with no expiry confers access FOREVER, and FR-33 holds only for BFF-written rows. Users hold Create on `sprk_externalrecordaccess`, so the path is reachable today, not hypothetical.</cost-of-doing-nothing>
  </justification>

  <outputs>
    <output type="code">src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Infrastructure/ExternalAccess/ExternalParticipationService.cs</output>
    <output type="test">tests/integration/auth/UnifiedAccessControl/ (inverted characterization + the new predicate/mirror agreement test)</output>
    <output type="doc">entity-schema.md, EXTERNAL-ACCESS-ADMIN-SETUP.md, notes/task-107-grant-expiry-read-default.md</output>
  </outputs>

  <acceptance-criteria>
    <criterion testable="true">`ExpiryPredicate(today)` no longer contains `eq null`; a grant row with a null `sprk_expiresdate` is excluded by the filter.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">`ConfersAccessOn(null, today)` returns FALSE, and a test pins `ExpiryPredicate` and `ConfersAccessOn` to the same answer for the null case — so the two definitions cannot drift apart again.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Unchanged in the other direction: a row with an expiry in the future confers access, and a row expiring TODAY still confers access (`ge`, not `gt` — task 007's DateOnly rule).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Negative: rows the BFF creates are unaffected — since task 097 it always sends an expiry.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Every characterization test asserting the retired "null never expires" rule is INVERTED (and renamed where its name stated the rule), each carrying a one-line reason citing D-1. None was deleted. State the exact count changed.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Perturbation: restoring the `eq null` branch reddens the new tests, and restoring the mirror's null-passthrough reddens the agreement test INDEPENDENTLY. Both reverted; exact pass/fail counts reported for each.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">No Dataverse plugin, business rule or schema default was added anywhere — `src/dataverse/**` is untouched by this task (verified by diff).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">entity-schema.md no longer says expiry is enforced nowhere; EXTERNAL-ACCESS-ADMIN-SETUP.md carries the pre-deploy COUNT command and states that undated active grants lose access on deploy.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Build green; full external-access suite green with exact numbers; publish size reported against a FRESH master build.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>Register ISS-009; GitHub #974 (label unified-access-control-r2). Related: tasks 097, 100, 101; DEF-001 (#961). Siblings from the same D-1 decision: **117** (option B — the scheduled reconciliation job) and **118** (option C — retire the Create-privilege proxy, code before config).

🔴 RESCOPED 2026-09-21 as a task-115 follow-on. This file was NOT in task 115's nine, so its premise rot survived that pass — it is the TENTH instance. What it said before: build a Dataverse-side default (plugin or entity-scope business rule), with acceptance criterion 5 requiring that *"the read filter is unchanged (grep: the `eq null` clause is still there)"* — the exact opposite of the owner's decision, which is to remove that clause. Its `&lt;parallel-safe&gt;` was also flipped true → **false**: the rescope moved it from `src/dataverse/**` into `Infrastructure/ExternalAccess/**`, the project's exclusive zone.

The single most important thing this file now carries and did not before: **`ConfersAccessOn` (`:124-125`) is the in-memory mirror of `ExpiryPredicate` and must change with it.** Changing only the predicate makes `/grant` report a grant as live while the reader denies it.</notes>

